{"title":"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the circumcenter and the orthocenter of an acute triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> lie on side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/7/b074e07e78260da01afd38e793d720310ca46253.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BM = CM$\" width=\"92\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/a/d6a079d215c7da2b80db30ac3feb4818f852ccf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAD = \\angle CAD$\" width=\"136\" height=\"13\" />. Ray <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/e/4fe8176d34b11c58cdf7b40a9decdb390f934318.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MO$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> intersects the circumcircle of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/e/14ef80efa9fd67f322d3769f5d69395d9a762fef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BHC$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> in point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/b/3cb711d0353bc247d4dfd55dd57bfd123e5426f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADO = \\angle HAN$\" width=\"140\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d8f0d7f5d1982ebe2f5f73bce4706b18b03d1a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]     size(12cm); import geometry; import olympiad;     pair O = (0, 0);     pair A = (-0.5, 0.86);     pair B = (-0.9, -0.44);     pair C = (0.9, -0.44);     pair D = (-0.15, -0.44);     pair H = (-0.5, -0.02);     pair N = (0, 0.11);     pair M = (0, -0.44);     pair P = (0, -1);     dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, N); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, W); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, E); dot(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, S); dot(&quot;$H$&quot;, H, S); dot(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, E); dot(&quot;$M$&quot;, M, SE); dot(&quot;$N$&quot;, N, NE); dot(&quot;$P$&quot;, P, NE);      draw(A--B--C--cycle); draw(N--P, grey); draw(A--H, grey); draw(A--N, grey); draw(A--P, grey); draw(D -- O, grey);     draw(circle(O, 1)); draw(circumcircle(A, O, D), dashed); draw(circumcircle(B, H, C), grey);     clip(box((-1, -1.05), (1, 1.05))); [/asy]\" width=\"555\" height=\"568\" />\n</p><p>It is well known that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/7/2a70267d24573862df6d04415c2424bdb6b3fb61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(BHC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> is the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/5155aa866a797f98b826ed02392324eb8d7c0b5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> (if you don't know this, consider the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />)\n</p><p>Thus, if we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/d/61d49344a361c134c33e925b23c5cf60f6953f35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overparen{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />. Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/3/c2378157b3d3f2c78fe4d7d08dcb4dfb54aa43c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, D, P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> are collinear. \n</p><p><b>Claim: </b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/8/7889e7957215c4c8817205d736f466bbcd158c16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ANOD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> is cyclic\n</p><p>Proof: We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/e/58e92b139f84fe99e769b53f195db31aaa672fdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DN=DP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/c/d3c753caeaa5fc9f14f22456e4d0813111756e81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OA=OP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c965523093bb3ede38ca245f54f398a020a95b2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle DNO = \\angle DPO = \\angle DAO\\quad \\blacksquare\\]\" width=\"250\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>To finish, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/8/fd88c28c09642b773d4318acb6af27ebaba4bcc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AH} \\parallel \\overline{NP}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"22\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/e/21e4333a928145ffeb4e1e9d00cefc7028c2f2ff.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle ADO = 180^\\circ - \\angle ANO = \\angle HAN \\quad \\square\\]\" width=\"308\" height=\"13\" />\n-sillybone\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f991461ea796e4eb31d0bdc0559d08a0d29cc38c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import olympiad;  unitsize(100);  pair pA = dir(120); pair pB = dir(225); pair pC = dir(315); pair pO = origin; pair pH = orthocenter(pA, pB, pC); pair pM = midpoint(pB--pC); pair dD = bisectorpoint(pB, pA, pC); pair pD = extension(pA, dD, pB, pC); pair pN = intersectionpoints(pM--(3*pO-2*pM), circumcircle(pB, pH, pC))[0]; pair dHprime = foot(pH, pB, pC); pair pHprime = 2*dHprime-pH; pair dAprime = foot(pA, pB, pC); pair pAprime = 2*dAprime-pA; pair dNprime = foot(pN, pB, pC); pair pNprime = 2*dNprime-pN;  draw(pA--pB--pC--cycle); draw(unitcircle, blue); draw(pH--pHprime, magenta); draw(pA--pD, red); draw(circumcircle(pB, pH, pC), blue); draw(pA--pH, blue); draw(pA--pN, magenta); draw(pA--pO, blue); draw(pD--pNprime, magenta);  dot(&quot;\\(A\\)&quot;, pA, NW); dot(&quot;\\(B\\)&quot;, pB, W); dot(&quot;\\(C\\)&quot;, pC, E); dot(&quot;\\(O\\)&quot;, pO, SW, blue); dot(&quot;\\(H\\)&quot;, pH, SW, blue); dot(&quot;\\(N\\)&quot;, pN, NE, magenta); dot(&quot;\\(M\\)&quot;, pM, S, red); dot(&quot;\\(D\\)&quot;, pD, S, red); dot(&quot;\\(H&#39;\\)&quot;, pHprime, SW, magenta); dot(&quot;\\(A&#39;\\)&quot;, pAprime, SW); dot(&quot;\\(N&#39;\\)&quot;, pNprime, S, magenta); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"572\" />\n(original diagram by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Integralarefun\" title=\"User:Integralarefun\">integralarefun</a>)\n</p><p>It's well known that the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/8/f783df024abc16be6be7f4d262c926e8f24fc6a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H&#39;$\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, lies on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/5155aa866a797f98b826ed02392324eb8d7c0b5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/7/2a70267d24573862df6d04415c2424bdb6b3fb61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(BHC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> is just the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/6/0b67617f2b0a61caf5284b0e38eddb1d6bd9e8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(BH&#39;C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"20\" /> across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, which is equivalent to the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/5155aa866a797f98b826ed02392324eb8d7c0b5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(ABC)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />. Reflect points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> to points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/9/1090530ae883b627e090bfb5e450ef796febca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/5/065e6d116d16109a19d183a52f7714325b6be623.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N&#39;$\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, respectively. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/5/065e6d116d16109a19d183a52f7714325b6be623.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N&#39;$\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of minor arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f997f2e439fbb9578ac4d715bc866bbea93ebc2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overarc{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"20\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/7/8a74d32997c2811a5cbe90b48277574bf16346e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, D, N&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"19\" /> are collinear in that order. It suffices to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/2/312dbe3d1b27f6deeeb3367b8e1eb6b6517c5e10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AA&#39;N&#39;=\\angle ADO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"148\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p><b>Claim:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/1/fb19fb8d855875ce053e9a0e0702aa7147f00924.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AA&#39;N&#39; \\sim \\triangle ADO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"19\" />. The proof easily follows.\n</p><p><b>Proof:</b> Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/5/9f5c022dbef61d254ad71069c0d0e8f752782771.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAA&#39;=\\angle CAO=90^{\\circ}-\\angle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"268\" height=\"17\" />. Then we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/a/03ab105f994bb8a864211ad43c45885608fae861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A&#39;AN&#39;=\\angle BAD-\\angle BAA&#39;=\\angle CAD-\\angle CAO=\\angle DAO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"469\" height=\"17\" />. So, it suffices to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/e/fde62cc468a9af73a5d9a027c55b732f0905add0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{AA&#39;}{AN&#39;}=\\frac{AD}{AO}\\rightarrow AA&#39;\\cdot AO=AN&#39;\\cdot AD.\\]\" width=\"300\" height=\"38\" /> Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/6/2e6a9fdb629cd283d7f25f4878eee986de35d680.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABA&#39; \\sim \\triangle AOC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"19\" />, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce0e9c4e792cea36f9f23fc3383038a41c922a64.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{AB}{AA&#39;}=\\frac{AO}{AC}\\rightarrow AA&#39;\\cdot AO=AB\\cdot AC.\\]\" width=\"287\" height=\"37\" /> Therefore, it suffices to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/7/4f758bc83a69e53adb0926b35cd654286b52a486.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AB\\cdot AC=AN&#39;\\cdot AD\\rightarrow \\frac{AB}{AN&#39;}=\\frac{AD}{AC}.\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"37\" /> But it is easy to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/d/03dff1e7ff600361b9852070020ffd6adb32aa1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BAN&#39;\\sim \\triangle DAC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"151\" height=\"19\" />, implying the result. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d020881fdcfde6b8569efe1136703380b1878f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(9cm); pair A = dir(130);  pair B = dir(220);  pair C = dir(320);  draw(unitcircle, lightblue);  pair P = dir(-90);  pair Q = dir(90); pair D = extension(A, P, B, C);  pair O = origin;  pair M = extension(B, C, O, P);  pair N = 2*M-P;  draw(A--B--C--cycle, lightblue);  draw(A--P--Q, lightblue);  draw(A--N--D--O--A, lightblue);  draw(A--D--N--O--cycle, red);  dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, dir(A));  dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, dir(B));  dot(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, dir(C));  dot(&quot;$P$&quot;, P, dir(P));  dot(&quot;$Q$&quot;, Q, dir(Q));  dot(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, dir(225));  dot(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, dir(315));  dot(&quot;$M$&quot;, M, dir(315));  dot(&quot;$N$&quot;, N, dir(315)); [/asy]\" width=\"378\" height=\"428\" />\n</p><p>Suppose ray <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2f5d57528aa7eeb9d5080cb5c25b4b06192862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OM$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> intersects the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/e/14ef80efa9fd67f322d3769f5d69395d9a762fef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BHC$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/5/065e6d116d16109a19d183a52f7714325b6be623.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N&#39;$\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and let the foot of the A-altitude of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/9/269acbb5c833ffea8fa6c53e71ef6a9576e4bf66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BHE=90-\\angle HBE=90-90+\\angle C=\\angle C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"365\" height=\"14\" />. Likewise, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/4/0640f44a78008b9df91eb067718a7a909d2bbb5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CHE=\\angle B$\" width=\"109\" height=\"12\" />. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52ffbe87a52437ade50e30bdea64e4355f305a53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BHC=\\angle BHE+\\angle CHE=\\angle B+\\angle C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"322\" height=\"14\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/8/d881e7199322ba96db9f897a7c85d30b13d4ed2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BHCN&#39;$\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" /> is cyclic, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/3/663b696e0260562921c8334fa14f9db0fe733f42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BN&#39;C=180-\\angle BHC=180-\\angle B-\\angle C=\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"396\" height=\"14\" />. Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/e/1ce9adfa61b9407846afb9713e4a2194796ee258.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC=\\angle A$\" width=\"106\" height=\"13\" />. These two angles are on different circles and have the same measure, but they point to the same line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />! Hence, the two circles must be congruent. (This is also a well-known result)\n</p><p>We know, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2f5d57528aa7eeb9d5080cb5c25b4b06192862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OM$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> is perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb3ddf9a5fa2fca5c4787d4f989af0eef1387bf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AH$\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" /> is also perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, so the two lines are parallel. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/a/9aafc7e3cc1eafce5e16d3a45250eeda7b1a6526.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AN$\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" /> is a transversal, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/318b2b169e7092840cfd76641ead16ecf5c8d679.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle HAN=\\angle ANO$\" width=\"140\" height=\"13\" />. We wish to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/1/2d12f38c532b992e3bf940b7a982c38739d4d7c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ANO=\\angle ADO$\" width=\"137\" height=\"13\" />, which is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd1d45c139c8abdae882f5dac5d0a1ddbbcbce78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AOND$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> being cyclic.\n</p><p>Now, assume that ray <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2f5d57528aa7eeb9d5080cb5c25b4b06192862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OM$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> intersects the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> at a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> must be the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/4/6f41b87801570e05b189ee17a3dc9e2a67bf30fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\stackrel{\\frown}{BC}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"20\" />. Also, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is an angle bisector, it must also hit the circle at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. The two circles are congruent, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/b/0ab08404a70174e2ae0363d8b52415e65c0c69d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN=MP\\implies ND=DP\\implies$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"268\" height=\"13\" /> NDP is isosceles. Angle ADN is an exterior angle, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/6/6a6d0fcf920a488a0ce79e1a47c427052ff9939f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADN=\\angle DNP+\\angle DPO=2\\angle DPO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"310\" height=\"14\" />. \nAssume WLOG that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/4/5a4b76583929c4838174fc8dea08eb882f41e8bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B&gt;\\angle C$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"79\" height=\"13\" />. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/3/c93f8ce99ad059b0b7e2db080d657a61ea5b3261.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DPO=\\angle APO=\\frac{\\angle B+\\angle C}{2}-\\angle C=\\frac{\\angle B-\\angle C}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"394\" height=\"37\" />.\nIn addition, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/9/929837d722aa965da76dfc9ab8a7ea26f82d3d6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AON=\\angle AOP=\\angle AOB+\\angle BOP=2\\angle C+\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"404\" height=\"14\" />. Combining these two equations, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/2/8920e1d9f55b7faf3604df0ec468dc6557461ea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AON+\\angle ADN=\\angle B-\\angle C+2\\angle C+\\angle A=\\angle A+\\angle B+\\angle C=180$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"547\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Opposite angles sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb1b41850ab676e4d1cf15c69b1609af4befbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />, so quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd1d45c139c8abdae882f5dac5d0a1ddbbcbce78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AOND$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> is cyclic, and the condition is proved.\n</p><p>-william122\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d63221273172dc2a4e61c00d1c27a091da6724c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(10cm); currentpen = fontsize(9pt);  pair A = dir(120); pair B = dir(220); pair C = dir(-40); pair O = (0,0); pair P = dir(-90); pair D = extension(A, P, B, C); pair M = extension(B, C, O, P); pair N = 2*M - P; pair H = A + B + C;  dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, dir(A)); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, W); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, E); dot(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, E); dot(&quot;$P$&quot;, P, dir(P)); dot(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, SW); dot(&quot;$M$&quot;, M, SE); dot(&quot;$N$&quot;, N, SE, orange + fontsize(9pt)); dot(&quot;$H$&quot;, H, S);  draw(unitcircle); draw(A--B--C--A); draw(O--P); draw(A--P); draw(A--H, dashed + gray); draw(B--P); draw(circumcircle(B,H,C), orange); draw(circumcircle(A,D,O), dashed + blue); draw(A--N, dashed + gray); draw(D--O, dashed + gray);  draw(anglemark(P, B, D), red); draw(anglemark(B, A, D), red); [/asy]\" width=\"475\" height=\"462\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the midpoint of the arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> not containing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. It is well-known that lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2f5d57528aa7eeb9d5080cb5c25b4b06192862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OM$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Additionally, the circumcircles of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/7/ce7cb063ae41e0bc7961b843186f600be909ac87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BHC$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> are known to be identical. (This property can be exhibited by showing that the circumradii of both circles are equivalent via the extended sine law.)\n</p><p>This means that the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/7/ce7cb063ae41e0bc7961b843186f600be909ac87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BHC$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> is the reflection of the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> about line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the reflection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> across <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/f/99f9b09eac5bb37f279692c70f4e8a80ff2b787d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PN$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" />; <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1ea23a597c2f7a68d03a209d1e232180b7d118b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PN = 2PM$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>As is the intent of Solution 2, the crucial finding deserving of the pedestal is the observation that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a05aaf3e949b499cfc8476ef8e9653858522d6d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADNO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> is a cyclic quadrilateral. Distinctly, to prove it, we will show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82cb21f1fd29e38cbdcd1826a97e3e6b5e321369.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PD\\cdot PA = PN\\cdot PO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"169\" height=\"14\" /> so that the converse of the intersecting secants theorem validates the observation claimed.\n</p><p>Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/0/1a0ebf28752782675ef381df4fb49212cefe5249.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle PBC = \\angle PAC = \\angle PAB = \\angle BAC/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"314\" height=\"20\" />. Consequently, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/b/a1b17398a891a0eac055b340f178ba2308b21909.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle PBD\\sim\\triangle PAB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"145\" height=\"18\" /> as both are equiangular. Evaluating their ratios suggests\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/f/9af3e4a8e2a08a3d086f10306983ecacd9252aa6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{PD}{PB} = \\frac{PB}{PA} \\Longrightarrow PD\\cdot PA = PB^2\\tag{1}\\]\" width=\"397\" height=\"37\" />\nand assuming <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/131a0ffc5c7a6fe180d86642e28aab99a2c83a5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R = OB = OP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"122\" height=\"14\" /> is the circumradius of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, by applications of Pythagoras’ theorem,\n\\begin{align*}PB^2 &amp;= PM^2 + BM^2 \\\\ &amp;= PM^2 + R^2 - OM^2 \\\\ &amp;= R^2 + (PM - OM)\\underbrace{(PM + OM)}_R \\\\ &amp;= R(R + PM - OM) \\\\ &amp;= R(PM + PM) \\\\ &amp;= R\\cdot PN \\\\ &amp;= PO\\cdot PN\\tag{2}\\end{align*}\nTherefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82cb21f1fd29e38cbdcd1826a97e3e6b5e321369.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PD\\cdot PA = PN\\cdot PO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"169\" height=\"14\" /> is indeed true, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a05aaf3e949b499cfc8476ef8e9653858522d6d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADNO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> is concyclic. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/e/60ed020e037834e10ccbcc412e3386653ec9e847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\checkmark$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>At last, we can complete the proof through a walk in the park. As both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/f/83f45521089cad2bd0059b0b60a917e59582cb7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AH,ON\\perp BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/4/b842f4fd277a0616a6fe80f1734a24608ef56c7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AH \\parallel ON$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/c/8ac1eadb8cdb3d5a70bc3b6ce7da47dd56218355.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle HAN = \\angle ANO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"143\" height=\"14\" />. Furthermore, the inscribed angle theorem in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a05aaf3e949b499cfc8476ef8e9653858522d6d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADNO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"14\" /> implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/e/a6e98fa56fd68e660a19d00a4f5585022e3dd8d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ANO = \\angle ADO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"140\" height=\"14\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c6e4f8059d1565ef809e2c0624a419735840f4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\boxed{\\therefore\\angle HAN = \\angle ADO}\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"24\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~jxyrx_\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO\" title=\"2017 USAJMO\">2017 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2017\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2017_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2017 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018004132\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.051 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.067 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 702/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 991/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 